1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:05,314
Hello! Today, we're going to learn about aspirated and tense consonants.

2
00:00:05,314 --> 00:00:11,390
These are sounds that make Hangeul richer. Shall we start together?

3
00:00:12,090 --> 00:00:21,964
Last time, we learned the soft plain consonants 'ㄱ', 'ㄷ', 'ㅂ', 'ㅈ', 'ㅅ'.

4
00:00:21,964 --> 00:00:27,106
These were sounds made by relaxing your throat and mouth.

5
00:00:27,106 --> 00:00:32,660
Today, we'll see how these friends transform.

6
00:00:33,360 --> 00:00:39,520
The first friend is the aspirated sound. It's a powerful sound where you forcefully exhale air from your mouth.

7
00:00:39,520 --> 00:00:45,980
Feel the cool air coming out of your mouth, as if you're exhaling hot breath.

8
00:00:46,680 --> 00:00:55,642
Aspirated sounds are made by adding a stroke to a plain consonant. Add a stroke to "ㄱ" and it becomes "ㅋ"!

9
00:00:55,642 --> 00:01:02,520
The soft "ㄱ" becomes a much stronger and more aspirated sound with just one stroke.

10
00:01:03,220 --> 00:01:15,266
In the same way, "ㄷ" becomes "ㅌ", "ㅂ" becomes "ㅍ", and "ㅈ" becomes "ㅊ".

11
00:01:15,266 --> 00:01:24,080
As strokes are added, the pronunciation becomes stronger, as if air is bursting forth.

12
00:01:24,780 --> 00:01:35,171
Shall we practice the 'ㅋ' sound? Forcefully push air from your throat. It's the 'ㅋ' in '코' (nose).

13
00:01:35,171 --> 00:01:41,320
If you put your palm in front of your mouth, you'll feel hot air.

14
00:01:42,020 --> 00:01:46,825
Next are 'ㅌ', 'ㅍ', 'ㅊ'.

15
00:01:46,825 --> 00:01:55,954
Pronounce them by exhaling air clearly, like in '타조' (ostrich), '포도' (grape), '치마' (skirt).

16
00:01:55,954 --> 00:02:01,720
All of them are sounds where air bursts out strongly.

17
00:02:02,420 --> 00:02:09,206
The second friend is the tense sound. It's a firm sound made by tightening your throat.

18
00:02:09,206 --> 00:02:13,580
Try to close your throat as if to prevent air from escaping.

19
00:02:14,280 --> 00:02:22,497
Tense sounds are made by placing two plain consonants side by side. Two 'ㄱ's together make 'ㄲ'!

20
00:02:22,497 --> 00:02:28,340
It becomes a stronger and firmer sound, like clenching both hands together.

21
00:02:29,040 --> 00:02:44,645
Similarly, 'ㄷ' becomes 'ㄸ', 'ㅂ' becomes 'ㅃ', 'ㅅ' becomes 'ㅆ', and 'ㅈ' becomes 'ㅉ'.

22
00:02:44,645 --> 00:02:53,880
As the letters double, the sound gains strength.

23
00:02:54,580 --> 00:03:03,185
Let's practice the 'ㄲ' sound. Tighten your throat and make the sound as if blocking the air.

24
00:03:03,185 --> 00:03:11,160
It's the 'ㄲ' in '까치' (magpie). You'll barely feel any air on your palm.

25
00:03:11,860 --> 00:03:18,303
Now for 'ㄸ', 'ㅃ', 'ㅆ', 'ㅉ'.

26
00:03:18,303 --> 00:03:28,868
Feel the firm sounds as you pronounce '꼬리' (tail), '땅' (ground), '빵' (bread), '짜' (salty).

27
00:03:28,868 --> 00:03:34,280
All of them are sounds made with tension in the throat.

28
00:03:34,980 --> 00:03:40,900
Shall we try a fun experiment? Hold a piece of paper in front of your mouth and pronounce.

29
00:03:40,900 --> 00:03:44,544
Aspirated sounds make the air burst out and move the paper,

30
00:03:44,544 --> 00:03:51,830
while tense sounds hold the air firmly, so the paper barely moves. That's the biggest difference between the two sounds.

31
00:03:52,530 --> 00:03:56,062
Now, shall we compare the three sounds?

32
00:03:56,062 --> 00:04:03,542
'개' (dog), '캐' (dig), '깨' (sesame) sound similar, but the amount of air is different.

33
00:04:03,542 --> 00:04:09,568
'개' is pronounced comfortably, '캐' bursts out air strongly,

34
00:04:09,568 --> 00:04:18,710
and '깨' is made firmly with tension in the throat. Try it yourself and feel the difference.

35
00:04:19,410 --> 00:04:27,186
Today, we learned about aspirated and tense sounds in Hangeul. Practice pronunciation little by little every day.

36
00:04:27,186 --> 00:04:30,260
See you next time. Goodbye!
